    "innovation": "This paper reviews and compiles 30 years of physical and chemical data to derive global-size distribution parameters and inorganic particle composition of marine aerosols on a coarse 15\u00b0 \u00d7 15\u00b0 grid, creating a database for evaluating chemical transport models.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Differential Mobility Particle Sizers",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "particle size distribution",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Only data that were taken from instrumentation calibrated with respect to geometric size were included. Thus, much optical particle counter data was not included. Only data taken at a measured relative humidity of less than 40% RH were accepted as a matter of minimising the effect of hydration on the size distributions. This then reduced the data set largely to distributions determined via mobility or aerodynamic sizing techniques. (Differential Mobility Particle Sizers, DMPS (Hoppel, 1978) and Aerodynamic Particle Sizers, APS (Agarwal et al., 1982.)",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
